<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="connect-cli-agent"
      updated="August 23, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"stdio-bridge", "Set up a stdio client"},
        {"connect-later", "Connect a client you install later"},
        {"manual-setup", "Set up a client by hand"},
        {"direct-http", "Direct HTTP — no bridge"},
        {"verify", "Verify the connection"},
        {"sign-in-grants", "What the sign-in grants"},
        {"troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="connect-cli-agent">
        <:dek>
          Connect a local AI client to your action catalog through direct HTTPS or the
          <.docs_inline_code>emisar-mcp</.docs_inline_code>
          stdio bridge.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Using Claude.ai or ChatGPT instead? Those connect over OAuth with no key or bridge to
        manage — see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/connect-claude-ai"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Connect a cloud LLM</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          An account with at least one runner online. Otherwise the agent signs in fine and finds
          an empty catalog with nothing to call. New here? Run the
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
            quickstart
          </.link>
          first, then come back to wire in your client.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.agent_skill_cta
        class="mt-6"
        skill="connect-llm"
        prompt="Connect this machine's coding agent to my emisar account over MCP — install the emisar-mcp bridge, register the client, and prove a run end to end. First ask me which client I use. Use the connect-llm skill: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AndrewDryga/emisar/main/skills/connect-llm/SKILL.md"
      >
        The <code class="text-zinc-300">connect-llm</code>
        skill walks your agent through this whole page.
      </.agent_skill_cta>

      <%!-- ============================================================ --%>
      <%!-- Stdio bridge                                                  --%>
      <%!-- ============================================================ --%>
      <section>
        <.docs_h2 id="stdio-bridge">Set up a stdio client</.docs_h2>

        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-zinc-400">
          Most local clients speak stdio. The
          <.docs_inline_code>emisar-mcp</.docs_inline_code>
          bridge is a small binary that sits in between the agent and emisar control plane. Your
          client starts it, and it passes each call to your account. Install it once per machine —
          every client uses the same one.
        </p>

        <.docs_h3 id="install-bridge">Install</.docs_h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-base text-zinc-300">
          The installer resolves the latest
          <.docs_inline_code>mcp-v*</.docs_inline_code>
          release, verifies its checksum, and installs
          <.docs_inline_code>emisar-mcp</.docs_inline_code>
          into
          <.docs_inline_code>/usr/local/bin</.docs_inline_code>
          on macOS and Linux, and
          <.docs_inline_code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Emisar\bin</.docs_inline_code>
          on 64-bit Windows:
        </p>
        <.os_docs_code phx-no-format detected={@detected_os}>
          <:tab
            os={:linux}
            label="Linux"
            copy_text="curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh | sudo bash"
          ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL <span class="text-brand-300">https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh</span> | sudo bash</:tab>
          <:tab
            os={:windows}
            label="Windows"
            copy_text="irm https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.ps1 | iex"
          ><span class="text-zinc-500">PS&gt;</span> irm <span class="text-brand-300">https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.ps1</span> | iex</:tab>
          <:tab
            os={:macos}
            label="macOS"
            copy_text="curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh | sudo bash"
          ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL <span class="text-brand-300">https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh</span> | sudo bash</:tab>
        </.os_docs_code>
        <.docs_verify_download
          tarball="emisar-mcp-<version>-linux-amd64.tar.gz"
          checksums="SHA256SUMS-MCP"
          component="mcp"
        />
        <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          The installer then runs <code>emisar-mcp connect</code>, which finds the supported
          clients on this machine and asks about each one. Browser approval creates one key for
          direct <code>emisar-mcp</code>
          commands and a separate key for each client you pick. Direct commands then work without
          shell-profile exports. Each client config is edited in place, so its comments, settings,
          and other MCP servers are untouched. See <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/mcp-reference#command-line"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Use MCP tools from the shell</.link>.
        </p>
        <.docs_callout kind={:note}>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-zinc-400">Targeting a signed-dispatch runner?</strong>
            If a runner enforces <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/signed-dispatch"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >signed dispatch</.link>, the bridge needs
            <.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_SIGNING_KEY</.docs_inline_code>
            and
            <.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_SIGNING_CERT</.docs_inline_code>
            in its environment. Put them wherever your client passes environment to the bridge: the
            <.docs_inline_code>env</.docs_inline_code>
            block in its config file, or another
            <.docs_inline_code>-e</.docs_inline_code>
            flag if you registered it with
            <.docs_inline_code>claude mcp add</.docs_inline_code>
            or <.docs_inline_code>grok mcp add</.docs_inline_code>.
            <.docs_inline_code>emisar signing init</.docs_inline_code>
            prints both values. The bridge signs each action dispatch. Keep both values secret and
            off the portal.
          </p>
        </.docs_callout>
      </section>

      <section>
        <.docs_h2 id="connect-later">Connect a client you install later</.docs_h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-zinc-400">
          <.docs_inline_code>emisar-mcp connect</.docs_inline_code>
          is an ordinary command, so a client you install next month does not need a reinstall.
          Run it with no arguments to be asked about each client it finds, or name one directly.
          A client that already carries an emisar entry is left alone:
        </p>
        <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell" copy_text="emisar-mcp connect --client zed"><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> emisar-mcp connect --client zed

Connect emisar

  Emisar CLI      credential verified
  Claude Code     already connected
  Cursor          already connected
  Codex CLI       already connected
  Zed             not connected

Approve this machine in your browser

  https://emisar.dev/activate?code=FKZQ-2418

If prompted, enter this code: FKZQ-2418

Sent the link to your default browser. If it did not open, use the link above.

Waiting for approval (Ctrl-C to cancel)…

  Zed             connected → /home/operator/.config/zed/settings.json

Restart any client you just connected so it picks up the new server.</.docs_code>
        <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          A rerun keeps the stored credential until the control plane rejects it. The rest of
          the command surface — <.docs_inline_code>--all</.docs_inline_code>, <.docs_inline_code>disconnect</.docs_inline_code>,
          <.docs_inline_code>--auto-permit</.docs_inline_code>
          — is in <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/mcp-reference#cli-clients"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >MCP CLI &amp; reference</.link>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <.docs_h2 id="manual-setup">Set up a client by hand</.docs_h2>
        <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          When the installer does not support your client, or you would rather edit the config
          yourself, open
          <a
            href={~p"/app/agents/connect"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Connect an agent</a>
          in the console and pick the client. It shows the exact config file or command for that
          client with your key and the endpoint already filled in — the key is shown once, so
          copy the snippet from there rather than from a docs page that cannot know it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <.docs_h2 id="direct-http">Direct HTTP — no bridge</.docs_h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-zinc-400">
          Any MCP client that supports Streamable HTTP and a custom authorization header can
          connect straight to emisar. There is no bridge binary to install.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base text-zinc-300">
          <li>
            Open
            <a
              href={~p"/app/agents/connect"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Connect an agent</a>
            and select <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-200">Custom</strong>.
            Create a dedicated key and copy it before leaving the page. The key is shown once.
          </li>
          <li>
            Add an MCP server to your client, choose the
            <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-200">Streamable HTTP</strong>
            transport, and give it these two values:
          </li>
        </ol>
        <.docs_value label="Server URL" copy_text="https://emisar.dev/api/mcp/rpc">
          https://emisar.dev/api/mcp/rpc
        </.docs_value>
        <.docs_value
          label="Authorization header"
          copy_text="Authorization: Bearer emk-..."
        >
          Authorization: Bearer emk-...
        </.docs_value>
        <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          Replace <code>emk-...</code> with the key you copied. Store it in the client's secret or
          user-level configuration. Do not commit it in a project configuration file.
        </p>

        <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="What direct HTTP leaves out">
          <p>
            A direct HTTP client cannot sign dispatches and does not rotate its agent key
            automatically. Use the stdio bridge when a runner requires <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/signed-dispatch"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >signed dispatch</.link>. Rotate or revoke a direct client's key from <a
              href={~p"/app/agents"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >AI agents</a>.
          </p>
        </.docs_callout>
      </section>

      <.docs_h2 id="verify">Verify the connection</.docs_h2>
      <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base text-zinc-300">
        <li>Restart or reconnect the client after its config changes.</li>
        <li>Confirm that the client lists the emisar MCP tools.</li>
        <li>
          Ask it to run <code>linux.uptime</code> on any one of the connected runners.
        </li>
        <li>Confirm that the client returns the action output.</li>
        <li>
          Open <a
            href={~p"/app/audit"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Audit</a>. Confirm that the event records the client, runner, action, and reason.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="sign-in-grants">What the sign-in grants</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Whether the installer minted it after your browser approval or you pasted one by hand, the
        key is bound to <em>your</em>
        membership. The agent acts as you. Every call is attributed to you and the agent you are using. It reaches only the
        runners and actions your own access already allows (<.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/agents-and-keys#no-scope"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >how a key inherits its operator's scope</.link>). Revoke it any time from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/agents"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >AI agents</a>
        in the console.
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/agents-and-keys"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          Manage agents & keys
        </.link>
        covers rotation.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_h2 id="mcp">The LLM client or the bridge</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Every call returns 401.</strong>
            The key expired, was revoked, or was retired when its rotation successor made its
            first call. A secret is shown only once and cannot be viewed again, so a failing
            client always needs a fresh one — rotating or minting is the fix, never a lookup. A
            revoked key cannot be restored. If the client never worked at all, check which token was
            copied: an audit-export token starts <.docs_inline_code>emk-export-</.docs_inline_code>, and the
            MCP endpoint refuses it as the wrong kind. Mint or rotate a key in
            <a
              href={~p"/app/agents"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >AI agents</a>
            and put the new secret in the client config. For a client the bridge configured,
            <.docs_inline_code>emisar-mcp connect --client &lt;id&gt;</.docs_inline_code>
            does both steps in one go.
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/agents-and-keys"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >Manage agents & keys</.link>
            covers scope and expiry.
            <.link navigate={~p"/docs/credentials"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Rotate and revoke credentials</.link>
            covers the cutover.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The bridge is outdated or unsupported.</strong>
            The
            <a
              href={~p"/app/agents"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >AI agents</a>
            page shows the version each key last reported, and
            <.docs_inline_code>emisar-mcp --version</.docs_inline_code>
            shows what is installed. A running client keeps the binary it already loaded, so
            restart the client after installing. See <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/bridge-upgrades"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >Upgrade the MCP bridge</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A tool call comes back with an error code.</strong>
            <.docs_inline_code>invalid_args</.docs_inline_code>
            returns the exact paths to correct, so fix those rather than retrying the same call.
            The complete code list, with the one action each implies, is in <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/mcp-reference"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >MCP CLI &amp; reference</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The response was lost after a mutation.</strong>
            Recover by operation ID with <.docs_inline_code>get_operation</.docs_inline_code>,
            using the same key that made the call — after a rotation, its successor counts. Never
            repeat the mutation. Cancelling only stops the bridge's wait and closing the client
            lets requests already in flight finish, so a cancelled call, closed client, or
            dropped bridge does not undo work the control plane already admitted.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Contact support</strong>
            when the same tool returns a transport error on every attempt. Also contact support when a
            recorded operation ID is missing under the credential that created it.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_cta
    headline="Point your agent at real infrastructure — safely."
    subcopy="Set up a runner first, then connect Claude Code, Cursor, or any MCP client to your account's endpoint and let policy decide what runs."
    secondary_label="Read the quickstart"
    secondary_path={~p"/docs/quickstart"}
  />

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
